{"round": 1, "phase": "full", "verdict": "ISSUES_FOUND", "blocking": true, "verifier_model": "gpt-5.4", "verifier_provider": "openai", "orchestrator_provider": "anthropic", "findings": [{"description": "Copilot verification can route to models the config explicitly marks as not trusted to verify.", "severity": "major", "category": "Correctness", "failureScenario": "On the shipped `copilot-cli` path, every verification call excludes the author's provider. For OpenAI-authored work, the shipped verifier order puts `gemini-3.1-pro-preview` ahead of Anthropic, and `resolve_role_candidates()` will select it whenever the seat confirms it, even though the model record marks that same model `is_enabled_as_verifier: false`. That means a normal cross-provider verification round can make merge/block decisions with a model this repo explicitly says is not trusted to judge other models' work.", "evidencePaths": ["ai_router/transports/copilot.py", "ai_router/router-config.yaml", "ai_router/router-config.yaml", "ai_router/router-config.yaml", "ai_router/selection.py"], "blocking": true, "section": "body"}, {"description": "`smoke.py` stopped exercising provider APIs after the default transport flipped to `copilot-cli`.", "severity": "major", "category": "Correctness", "failureScenario": "A developer with both a Copilot seat and provider keys runs `smoke.py` to validate Anthropic/OpenAI/Google API access. Because the shipped config now defaults to `copilot-cli` and the script calls `route(...)` without a transport override, each probe runs through the seat instead of the provider API. A green `[ok] openai` or `[ok] google` now proves only that the seat could answer through that provider family, not that the direct API key/config works, so broken API setup is likely to slip through until a real API-backed workflow fails.", "evidencePaths": ["smoke.py", "14-33", "ai_router/router-config.yaml", "ai_router/config.py"], "blocking": true, "section": "body"}], "completion_tree": "d6d92f933